WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.869 --> 00:00:05.200
Not everyone makes it out of The Walking Dead
alive: the title of the popular zombie drama

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.200
could tell you that.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.560
But the most recent episode, the penultimate
installment of season 9, featured some especially

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.179
grisly deaths that left viewers shocked.

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:14.179
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:14.179 --> 00:00:18.550
Episode 15 of Season 9, "The Calm Before,"
revealed that the Whisperers and their leader

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:23.630
Alpha killed 10 characters by decapitation,
then stuck their heads on spikes and organized

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:24.630
them in a row.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:28.360
"If the alpha doesn't assert herself, then
there's chaos."

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:32.469
This is exceptionally brutal stuff, even for
The Walking Dead, but the three murders that

00:00:32.469 --> 00:00:36.730
drew more gasps from viewers than the others
were those of Tara, Henry, and Enid.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:40.690
Both because their deaths proved just how
ruthless Alpha really is, and because those

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:45.020
characters don't die in the original version
of this storyline as seen in The Walking Dead

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:47.480
comics, Ezekiel and Rosita did.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:52.569
After the episode aired, The Walking Dead
director and co-executive producer Greg Nicotero

00:00:52.569 --> 00:00:57.440
opened up to The Hollywood Reporter about
the decision to kill Tara, Henry, and Enid

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.640
and the choice to deviate from the comic book
source material, saying,

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:04.809
"When we break the story, and when the writers
are in the writers room, we don't always know

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:05.910
when an event is coming.

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:09.721
A lot of times, we'll find out at the very
beginning of the season where some of the

00:01:09.721 --> 00:01:10.721
landmarks are.

00:01:10.721 --> 00:01:15.000
But we had no idea who it was going to be
or when it was going to be until probably

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.050
two or three episodes before we shot."

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:21.360
Nicotero says that the deaths felt "random
and uncontrollable," because Alpha didn't

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:26.090
choose to kill people based on any personal
vendetta against them: she killed to kill,

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:30.610
to send a message to the people of Alexandria,
the Hilltop, the Kingdom, the Sanctuary, and

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:31.610
Oceanside.

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:32.610
Nicotero continued,

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:36.860
"First of all, the deaths are at the hands
of a human being instead of walkers, so that

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:38.330
immediately makes it different…

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:42.480
You're constantly reminded that man's inhumanity
toward man is the most horrific thing that

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:43.480
can occur.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:44.670
There's a real brutality to it."

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:49.040
Nicotero revealed that the creative team had
to think about how the deaths in this episode

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.230
would affect other characters, and that pushed
them to pick Tara, Henry, and Enid.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:58.020
As he explained, there is a "very specific
reason to lose" Henry due to his relationship

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:02.700
with his adoptive parents Carol and Ezekiel,
as well as with Daryl Dixon.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:07.180
As far as Tara is concerned, her growing power
at the Hilltop influenced the decision to

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:08.180
kill her off.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:12.849
And in a somewhat similar vein, Enid's developing
relationship with Alden was also a motivating

00:02:12.849 --> 00:02:13.849
factor.

00:02:13.849 --> 00:02:17.989
The critical consensus regarding the brutal
episode has been positive, with Den of Geek

00:02:17.989 --> 00:02:22.890
calling "The Calm Before" quote, a "stellar
exercise in taking a television show and making

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:24.120
it look like an event."

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.980
CBR called it "easily one of the best episodes
of what's become one of The Walking Dead's

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:32.299
best seasons" thanks to the heads-on-spikes
sequence, which is bound to impact the future

00:02:32.299 --> 00:02:34.040
of the show in a massive way.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:38.620
Meanwhile, Nicotero commended Alpha actress
Samantha Morton on her chilling performance

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.730
in the episode, telling the Hollywood Reporter,

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:43.780
"Samantha has done such an amazing job this
season.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.599
She so embodies everything that the show needs
and wants."

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:50.230
Though these deaths will likely leave a hole
in the hearts of many Walking Dead loyals,

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:54.900
they do help to thin the very thick cast of
a series that isn't going anywhere any time

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:55.900
soon.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:03:00.879
AMC has already ordered a 10th season of the
show, with CEO Josh Sapan saying in September

00:03:00.879 --> 00:03:05.819
2018 that the network plans to keep the Walking
Dead IP alive for another decade through all

00:03:05.819 --> 00:03:11.019
sorts of media, from the flagship series to
spin-off shows to films and additional comic

00:03:11.019 --> 00:03:12.019
book issues.

00:03:12.019 --> 00:03:16.019
Cutting down the number of characters on screen
is a necessary evil, as it will allow the

00:03:16.019 --> 00:03:19.049
show to continue refining its focus moving
forward.

00:03:19.049 --> 00:03:23.330
We know it's difficult to say goodbye to characters,
some of whom have been around for years, but

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:28.159
perhaps these 10 new Walking Dead victims
had to fall so their fellow apocalypse survivors

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:29.040
could flourish.

